{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised. I will independently review the binding-replacement implementation at exact head 02e3b0c23138dc6e46c34abff0a8d79586251620 on request/replace-host-binding against e430878f, signing all four artifacts 208d9999, e1d396c8, 4ce835f9 and 506b13a2, and recording Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. I will not edit or merge.\n\nScope is the core half only, per my narrowing at ffa821db: host.ReplaceBinding and its verification, authority, idempotence, race behaviour, downstream sufficiency, tests and documentation. The chess operator surface is a separate commitment at 88979a1f in the chess workroom and is not part of this verdict.\n\nChecked before promising: 208d9999 at path host and 506b13a2 at docs/reference/architecture.md are both live, unretired and unstale at that commit, so a verdict resting on them can carry a merge. I will confirm the same for e1d396c8 and 4ce835f9 at the start of the review rather than assume it.\n\nWhat I will hold it to, beyond the gates at the exact head with -count=1. Authority is the thing I care most about, because this operation rewrites what a whole repository means. The signer must be proved to be the first-record actor from the log itself, not from a mutable role or a current config, since roles can be revoked between check and use and the initializing actor is an immutable fact. I will look specifically for whether that proof can be satisfied by anything a caller supplies. Verification must be genuine kernel verification of the repository rather than a structural read. Refusal of an identical binding matters because a no-op that reports success invites repetition, and refusal of a mismatched one is what stops a repository being moved somewhere it still cannot be read. The PostDedup recheck on every CAS attempt is the interesting one: I will check it is inside the retry rather than before it, because a binding validated once and then written after a lost race is exactly the hole that rule exists to close.\n\nDownstream sufficiency I will judge against the chess conditions I already wrote: chess must be able to sign with the key from its own store, learn what changed from what, and refuse rather than guess when the key is absent. If the API cannot support that, this is changes-requested even if every internal property holds, because the request exists to make a user-visible recovery possible.\n\nOne thing I will raise rather than block on unless it is worse than it looks: docs/reference/architecture.md now carries six live unretired artifacts including 506b13a2. That is a merge-time accounting problem, not a correctness one, but it will refuse the merge until every in-target predecessor is handled, and some of those are not codex's.\n\nSequenced after my current reserved-fields repair, which codex is waiting on, and after the chess display-names re-verdict."}